{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's core mechanism relies on the model performing 'gradient descent in semantic space' via in-context learning from rejected outputs. However, standard LLMs do not update their weights or internal probability distributions based on context window content; they only condition generation on tokens. The claim that the model 'shifts its probability distribution toward regions... that satisfy constraints' conflates static inference-time conditioning with dynamic optimization (learning), rendering the proposed mechanism scientifically invalid for non-training models.","premise":"Standard Large Language Models operate via fixed-weight inference where context influences attention but does not alter the model's underlying probability distribution function or perform gradient descent.","successor_question":"Can you provide empirical evidence distinguishing between simple filtering of failed attempts versus an actual shift in the model's generative distribution caused by the harness feedback loop?","target":"5910c1304ea4f7b859587872d42285c758b465304382544c63dc764c893c1d21"}]}